# ASPEED AST2050 (G3) family arch selects.
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# Mirrors soc/microchip/sam/sam9/Kconfig (the ARM926EJ-S precedent, PR #103557):
# the family pulls in the ARM AArch32 arch, the ARM926 CPU, a custom (non-GIC)
# interrupt controller, the MMU, and the C-based atomics (ARMv5TE has no
# LDREX/STREX).

config SOC_FAMILY_ASPEED_G3
	select ARM
	select CPU_ARM926EJ_S
	select ARM_CUSTOM_INTERRUPT_CONTROLLER
	select MMU
	select ATOMIC_OPERATIONS_C
	select SOC_RESET_HOOK
	# SOC_RESET_HOOK is REQUIRED, not optional: soc_reset_hook()
	# (soc/aspeed_g3/ast2050/soc.c) invalidates the ARM926 I/D-cache + TLB before
	# z_arm_mmu_init turns the MMU/caches on. Without it, on real silicon the
	# freshly-enabled D-cache serves stale power-on garbage and the image
	# data-aborts at __start (QEMU cannot catch this — it models no cache
	# contents). Select it explicitly here. This symbol is deliberately
	# SOC_FAMILY_ASPEED_G3, disjoint from upstream Zephyr's SOC_FAMILY_ASPEED
	# (the Cortex-M AST10x0/AST2600 family): if the names collided, Kconfig would
	# append both families' selects onto one symbol.
	# MMU is required by the ARM9 arch (CONFIG_MMU stays y even if not
	# selected here). The port RUNS AN APPLICATION under QEMU (evidence
	# d14-zephyr/03): "*** Booting Zephyr OS ***" + "Hello World!" via a
	# static-mapped physical-address SoC console (soc/aspeed_g3/ast2050/console.c,
	# printk+stdout hooks), sidestepping the brand-new upstream ARM9 arm_mmu not
	# translating the ns16550 z_phys_map device VA. The M1 VIC + aspeed timer
	# (vic.c/aspeed_timer.c) deliver interrupts; sustained tickful scheduling
	# awaits the upstream arm_mmu dynamic-mapping fix.
